Testing Your API — Exercise: Design tests for PATCH /tasks/{id}

`PATCH /tasks/{id}` updates only the fields sent. Rules: `title`, if present, must be 1–200 characters; `done`, if present, must be a boolean; no other fields are allowed.

Write a test plan table with at least eight rows (case, request, expected status, and what to check in the database) covering the happy path, boundary values for `title`, a wrong type, an unknown field, a missing task, a missing token, and Alice trying to patch Bob's task 77. Then explain how you would keep these tests independent of each other, and which level of the test pyramid a pure check of the 200-character rule belongs to.

Designing REST Endpoints — Worked example: PUT vs PATCH on the same task

Task 42 starts as `{"title": "Buy milk", "done": false, "due_date": "2026-10-20"}`. Bob's app sends the same small body two ways.

```http
PATCH /v1/tasks/42
{"done": true}
-> 200 {"title": "Buy milk", "done": true, "due_date": "2026-10-20"}

PUT /v1/tasks/42
{"title": "Buy milk", "done": true}
-> 200 {"title": "Buy milk", "done": true, "due_date": null}

PUT /v1/tasks/42
{"done": true}
-> 422 {"error": {"code": "validation_failed",
        "message": "title is required"}}
```

(Headers are trimmed to fit.) Reading the results:

1. PATCH changed one field; everything else was kept.
2. PUT without `due_date` replaced the task and the due date was wiped, because the body is the new whole.
3. PUT without `title` was rejected: a complete task must have a title.

Rule of thumb: use PATCH for small edits such as ticking a checkbox; use PUT when the client has the full edited form and wants to save all of it.
